# Persistence conventions

- Keep database access inside infrastructure adapters.
- Implement existing repository interfaces rather than introducing
  parallel ways to access the same data.
- Map database records to domain objects explicitly when needed.
- Never expose ORM model types as domain or application contracts.
- Do not put business rules in repository implementations.
- Use database transactions when a use case requires multiple
  writes to succeed or fail atomically.
- Keep schema changes and migrations consistent with the
  project's existing database workflow.
- Do not silently change persisted data formats or API contracts.
- Treat external input as untrusted, even when it reaches
  the persistence layer through an application use case.
- Add tests for important mapping, persistence, and transaction
  behavior where practical.
- Avoid generic repository frameworks unless there is a
  demonstrated need for them.
